1
00:00:09,620 --> 00:00:10,449
Hello.

2
00:00:10,449 --> 00:00:14,905
All right, this time we're going to
talk about sharing the link in

3
00:00:14,905 --> 00:00:16,728
the link layer protocol.

4
00:00:16,728 --> 00:00:22,063
So as a reminder here, so
what problem is being solved

5
00:00:22,063 --> 00:00:27,519
now is that the transmission
mediums that we're going

6
00:00:27,519 --> 00:00:33,937
to be targeting can be shared
across multiple network devices.

7
00:00:33,937 --> 00:00:39,043
And so what we need is to be
able to coordinate between

8
00:00:39,043 --> 00:00:44,507
them to regulate the access
to this underlying medium.

9
00:00:44,507 --> 00:00:50,572
So what can happen if we don't
do this is interference, right?

10
00:00:50,572 --> 00:00:54,660
So here we have both a wired
network on the left and

11
00:00:54,660 --> 00:00:57,471
a wireless network on the right.

12
00:00:57,471 --> 00:01:05,444
So the wired network, this is actually
how Ethernet was originally created and

13
00:01:05,444 --> 00:01:11,640
imagined that you can create to
a single basically big cable.

14
00:01:11,640 --> 00:01:17,042
And then any computer that you wanted
to attach to it, you would clamp

15
00:01:17,042 --> 00:01:22,837
into that cable, and now they would
be on that effectively the same wire.

16
00:01:22,837 --> 00:01:27,845
And so you could see here that we
might have two different nodes,

17
00:01:27,845 --> 00:01:32,971
the green and the blue,
both trying to transmit at the same time.

18
00:01:32,971 --> 00:01:40,080
Likewise, in the wireless, we see blue and
green also trying to transmit.

19
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:45,168
So once the kind of signals
propagate far enough,

20
00:01:45,168 --> 00:01:50,255
whether it's on the same wire or
through the air,

21
00:01:50,255 --> 00:01:56,936
they start colliding with each other and
interference happens.

22
00:01:56,936 --> 00:02:02,476
And at this point,
no data is usefully transmitted.

23
00:02:02,476 --> 00:02:06,818
So this is where multiple access protocols

24
00:02:06,818 --> 00:02:11,287
come in as part of
the link layer protocols.

25
00:02:11,287 --> 00:02:18,380
So the key question is how do we share
the link fairly and efficiently?

26
00:02:18,380 --> 00:02:20,373
So there's different approaches here.

27
00:02:20,373 --> 00:02:25,366
So one possible approach is to
just partition up the channel.

28
00:02:25,366 --> 00:02:29,671
So divide the link into smaller pieces and

29
00:02:29,671 --> 00:02:33,730
assign each piece to different nodes.

30
00:02:33,730 --> 00:02:36,789
So that's one approach, and
we'll show examples of that.

31
00:02:36,789 --> 00:02:40,817
And then another approach is
to do it kind of randomly.

32
00:02:40,817 --> 00:02:44,784
So nodes transmit in
an uncoordinated manner and

33
00:02:44,784 --> 00:02:49,437
then just have to deal with
collisions when they happen.

34
00:02:49,437 --> 00:02:52,710
So let's look at the channel
partitioning first.

35
00:02:52,710 --> 00:02:59,946
So one example of this is partitioning
the channel in terms of time.

36
00:02:59,946 --> 00:03:03,412
So this is called time division,
multiple access.

37
00:03:03,412 --> 00:03:10,603
So here we can divide into
equal sized time slots.

38
00:03:10,603 --> 00:03:14,236
So 0 to, let's say it's 1 second,
but that's very long.

39
00:03:14,236 --> 00:03:18,654
But 0 to 1 second might be one and
then 1 second to 2 and

40
00:03:18,654 --> 00:03:21,674
then 2 seconds to three and so forth.

41
00:03:21,674 --> 00:03:26,717
So you then can take those and
assign those slots to nodes.

42
00:03:26,717 --> 00:03:33,911
So during the 0 to 1 slot,
that's when node one could transmit.

43
00:03:33,911 --> 00:03:40,132
Then during the 1 to 2 slot, that's when 2
can transmit and then 3 could transmit and

44
00:03:40,132 --> 00:03:45,112
then 4 could transmit and then cycle
back through each of them again.

45
00:03:45,112 --> 00:03:50,038
And so nodes in this case can
only send during their slots.

46
00:03:50,038 --> 00:03:57,820
And so let's say slots are big enough
time to be able to transmit 100 bytes.

47
00:03:57,820 --> 00:04:04,840
And if one, node 1 only has 10 bytes
to send, maybe 2 has 0 bytes to send,

48
00:04:04,840 --> 00:04:10,144
3 has 200 bytes to send and
4 has 150 bytes to send.

49
00:04:10,144 --> 00:04:14,905
This is what the transmission would
look like during one's time slot.

50
00:04:14,905 --> 00:04:20,901
It would transmit the 100 bytes that it
has or 10 bytes that it has to send and

51
00:04:20,901 --> 00:04:26,628
then waste the rest of the time,
the time slot for node 2, since it doesn't

52
00:04:26,628 --> 00:04:31,842
have anything to transmit,
the channel at that point would be idle.

53
00:04:31,842 --> 00:04:38,028
Node 3 would send its 100, but
because its time slot is up,

54
00:04:38,028 --> 00:04:44,000
even though it has more to send,
it would have to yield to 4.

55
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:47,323
Then they would both then
wait while node 1 and

56
00:04:47,323 --> 00:04:50,996
node 2 don't transmit
anything to get back to 3.

57
00:04:50,996 --> 00:04:56,516
So you can see that this is certainly
fair, it's dividing it equally,

58
00:04:56,516 --> 00:05:01,854
but it's not really an efficient
channel or an efficient protocol.

59
00:05:01,854 --> 00:05:06,654
We could also partition
the channel in terms of frequency.

60
00:05:06,654 --> 00:05:10,752
It's called frequency
division multiple access.

61
00:05:10,752 --> 00:05:15,655
So here we could divide
the frequency spectrum and

62
00:05:15,655 --> 00:05:19,048
sign each node its own frequency.

63
00:05:19,048 --> 00:05:23,644
And so this is showing
a cable which can support or

64
00:05:23,644 --> 00:05:28,017
has different frequency
waves coming through

65
00:05:28,017 --> 00:05:32,962
different bands, and
we can assign to each of them.

66
00:05:32,962 --> 00:05:37,903
This, of course, kind of reduces
the efficiency of the channel as

67
00:05:37,903 --> 00:05:42,771
well because you're not kind of
maximizing the ability to send.

68
00:05:44,544 --> 00:05:49,938
All right, so
now switching over to random multi-access

69
00:05:49,938 --> 00:05:54,561
protocols, there's kind
of three key questions

70
00:05:54,561 --> 00:05:59,530
that kind of have to be
addressed in those protocols.

71
00:05:59,530 --> 00:06:02,810
And we'll look at a couple of examples,
but

72
00:06:02,810 --> 00:06:07,253
just to look at these questions,
the first is when to send.

73
00:06:07,253 --> 00:06:10,753
Should you just send as a node anytime, or

74
00:06:10,753 --> 00:06:15,255
should you at least make
an attempt to not interfere?

75
00:06:15,255 --> 00:06:18,407
Then the second question
is how do you know

76
00:06:18,407 --> 00:06:22,113
if your transmission was
actually successful?

77
00:06:22,113 --> 00:06:25,766
Can I detect this as the node
that was sending it?

78
00:06:25,766 --> 00:06:30,485
Does the receiver have to
acknowledge it and so forth?

79
00:06:30,485 --> 00:06:34,546
And then what do I do if
the transmission actually failed?

80
00:06:34,546 --> 00:06:36,256
Should we resend it immediately?

81
00:06:36,256 --> 00:06:38,330
Should we wait some amount of time?

82
00:06:38,330 --> 00:06:44,358
And these are questions that the protocol
specifies and everybody agrees to.

83
00:06:44,358 --> 00:06:47,393
So let's look at the when to send.

84
00:06:47,393 --> 00:06:53,040
So we're going to look at
Carrier Sense Multiple Access or CSMA.

85
00:06:53,040 --> 00:06:57,164
So it's used in both Ethernet and Wi-Fi.

86
00:06:57,164 --> 00:07:02,064
So in this case, the when to send
is actually that the node will

87
00:07:02,064 --> 00:07:06,968
read from the channel just to see
if another node is sending and

88
00:07:06,968 --> 00:07:11,146
only transmit if it sensed
that the channel is idle.

89
00:07:11,146 --> 00:07:15,763
So in these pictures below,
say A wants to send,

90
00:07:15,763 --> 00:07:22,199
it would sense the channel, but
it would see B is actually sending.

91
00:07:22,199 --> 00:07:28,849
It would see that there is some activity
on that wire, and so A would not send.

92
00:07:28,849 --> 00:07:32,192
In the picture on the right,
when D wants to go ahead and

93
00:07:32,192 --> 00:07:36,280
send, it would kind of read from
the channel and then see nothing.

94
00:07:36,280 --> 00:07:40,078
There's no activities and
it would be able to send.

95
00:07:42,293 --> 00:07:49,251
So now the next part of that is knowing if
the transmission was actually successful,

96
00:07:49,251 --> 00:07:54,445
and so here's where Ethernet and
Wi-Fi diverge a little bit.

97
00:07:54,445 --> 00:07:59,212
So with the Ethernet we have
something called CSMA/CD,

98
00:07:59,212 --> 00:08:02,701
where the CD is for collision detection.

99
00:08:02,701 --> 00:08:07,861
So a property of the Ethernet, NICs or
network interface cards is that

100
00:08:07,861 --> 00:08:12,942
they can actually read at the same
time that they're transmitting.

101
00:08:12,942 --> 00:08:17,875
And so what they can do is if what
they read in is different than what

102
00:08:17,875 --> 00:08:22,380
they're sending,
that means that there's a collision.

103
00:08:22,380 --> 00:08:25,495
So in this case,
let's look at this picture.

104
00:08:25,495 --> 00:08:29,945
So it's sending the node A is
sending into the channel,

105
00:08:29,945 --> 00:08:33,079
whatever this green line represents.

106
00:08:33,079 --> 00:08:39,845
What it reads is the red,
which is all the kind of collided data.

107
00:08:39,845 --> 00:08:47,553
So if what it reads is different than
what it's sending, that's a collision.

108
00:08:47,553 --> 00:08:52,860
Now, after it detects the collision,
it'll jam the signal so

109
00:08:52,860 --> 00:08:59,692
transmit some fixed bit pattern to ensure
that everyone detects the collision.

110
00:08:59,692 --> 00:09:05,284
So as a question that might be having is
well in the previous slide we just said,

111
00:09:05,284 --> 00:09:11,134
well they detect, they read from the
channel to see if anybody's transmitting,

112
00:09:11,134 --> 00:09:15,246
but now we're saying that
they could actually collide.

113
00:09:15,246 --> 00:09:18,589
Well, that's because signals
take time to transmit.

114
00:09:18,589 --> 00:09:22,846
So you may read and
at that point it looks clear, and

115
00:09:22,846 --> 00:09:27,602
then you start talking,
the node starts transmitting,

116
00:09:27,602 --> 00:09:31,879
and it sees and
then a collision actually happens.

117
00:09:31,879 --> 00:09:37,412
So with Ethernet it does this
collision detection technique and

118
00:09:37,412 --> 00:09:41,518
then basically jams
the transmission medium.

119
00:09:41,518 --> 00:09:47,550
On the wireless side or Wi-Fi,
it does collision avoidance.

120
00:09:47,550 --> 00:09:52,333
So the challenge with wireless
is that the NICs cannot

121
00:09:52,333 --> 00:09:56,809
read while transmitting,
that's one problem.

122
00:09:56,809 --> 00:10:01,952
And then another problem is they actually
may be out of range of some nodes.

123
00:10:01,952 --> 00:10:07,779
So maybe we have a set-up
like this where we have X and

124
00:10:07,779 --> 00:10:14,280
Z, maybe those are laptops and
then Y is the access point.

125
00:10:14,280 --> 00:10:17,271
Well, X is in range of Y,

126
00:10:17,271 --> 00:10:22,112
Z is in range of Y, but X and Z can't act,

127
00:10:22,112 --> 00:10:27,684
they're not actually in
range of each other.

128
00:10:27,684 --> 00:10:33,779
So even if they may be transmitting,
they both might be transmitting to Y.

129
00:10:33,779 --> 00:10:37,114
They wouldn't be able
to actually hear that.

130
00:10:37,114 --> 00:10:42,628
So in this case, the collision
avoidance part is that it actually

131
00:10:42,628 --> 00:10:47,656
depends on an explicit
acknowledgment from the receiver.

132
00:10:47,656 --> 00:10:52,737
So if it doesn't receive an acknowledgment
in a certain period of time,

133
00:10:52,737 --> 00:10:56,585
that's when it can assume
that there was a collision.

134
00:10:56,585 --> 00:11:03,055
And so we can see right around
why there can be a collision.

135
00:11:03,055 --> 00:11:11,488
Now, there is this option to where the
transmitter may submit a request to send,

136
00:11:11,488 --> 00:11:16,448
and then the receiver
says you're clear to send

137
00:11:16,448 --> 00:11:21,656
because your Y in this case
is in the range of both,

138
00:11:21,656 --> 00:11:26,137
and they can say at Y it's actually clear.

139
00:11:26,137 --> 00:11:30,721
So that's Wi-Fi, and that's how we
know if a transmission was successful.

140
00:11:32,260 --> 00:11:37,916
Now the final piece is what do
we do when a transmission fails?

141
00:11:37,916 --> 00:11:44,606
So here we're going to want to
retransmit the frame some time period,

142
00:11:44,606 --> 00:11:51,062
after some time period, and
this is called the back off period.

143
00:11:51,062 --> 00:11:54,070
And so why we want to do this is, well,

144
00:11:54,070 --> 00:11:59,242
we want to make sure that the receiver
actually gets the data, and

145
00:11:59,242 --> 00:12:04,343
we want to back off because we
want to allow the channel to clear up.

146
00:12:04,343 --> 00:12:08,157
And in both cases, the CSMA/CA and

147
00:12:08,157 --> 00:12:13,763
CD specifies that this back
off time will be random.

148
00:12:13,763 --> 00:12:18,600
And why it's random is if they both
were synchronized in the amount of time

149
00:12:18,600 --> 00:12:22,517
that they backed off,
they're just going to collide again.

150
00:12:22,517 --> 00:12:27,311
And so if they both choose something
random, whoever chose the lower

151
00:12:27,311 --> 00:12:32,365
number is the one that actually would
kind of be able to kind of transmit.

152
00:12:32,365 --> 00:12:38,105
And then the other one would detect
the channel has activity on it,

153
00:12:38,105 --> 00:12:42,731
and we'll wait till that
transmission is complete.

154
00:12:42,731 --> 00:12:48,099
But in the case where there
is continued collision,

155
00:12:48,099 --> 00:12:52,866
this back off time
exponentially increases.

156
00:12:52,866 --> 00:12:56,583
So you wait longer and longer each time.

157
00:12:56,583 --> 00:13:02,320
All right, so with that,
we covered how to share the channel,

158
00:13:02,320 --> 00:13:09,591
and so next time, we're going to talk
about expanding into a local area network.